## Agent process

### Issue tracker

Issues live in GitLab and are worked with `glab`. See `docs/agents/issue-tracker.md` (conventions, board, milestones, releases).

### Triage labels

`needs-triage`, `needs-info`, `ready-for-agent`, `ready-for-human`, `wontfix`, plus `idea`. See `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.

### Domain docs

`CONTEXT.md` (glossary) and `docs/adr/` at the repo root. See `docs/agents/domain.md`.

### Repo profile

How to check, build and publish this repo, and what agents must not do here: `docs/agents/repo-profile.md`. Its **Checks** section is what the implementer and checks agents run. Full suites run in GitLab CI on every MR; locally, run only the checks and tests that cover the change.

## Working economically

- **One issue per conversation.** When an issue's MR is open or a milestone is closed, stop and give a two- or three-line handoff so the session can be cleared.
- **Delegate routine work to cheaper agents** (`team-process` plugin):
  - `implementer` (Sonnet) for clearly specified code changes. The main conversation shapes the change and writes the plan.
  - `checks` (Haiku) to run checks or watch an MR's pipeline once, instead of polling.
  - `chores` (Haiku) for board, label, milestone and release-notes bookkeeping.

  Do tiny edits inline: a spawned agent starts cold, so it only pays off for real work or noisy output.
- Read only the parts of files you need; grep before reading. Filter command output to the lines that matter. Keep replies short. Follow narrow asks literally.

## Hard rules

- Gate every verify-then-commit step on **exit codes**, never on `| tail` output (a piped tail exits 0 and hides failures). State test counts only from the branch actually tested.
- After a routine merge, confirm it landed on the default branch and stop; don't poll deploy pipelines unless the change touches data, migrations or deploy scripts, or you're asked.
- Never close a milestone just to tidy up; it starts a release. Never create a release, push a tag or publish to Artifactory unless a human asked.
- Never build from an `idea`; shape it into `ready-*` tickets first.
- **Changes to infrastructure, CI/CD variables, protected branches, Artifactory repositories or permissions need explicit human approval first.** Propose the change and how to roll it back, then wait. Read-only lookups are fine.
- Never commit tokens, `.npmrc`/`settings.xml`/`gradle.properties` credentials or Artifactory API keys. Secrets come from `<vault / CI variables>`.
- Never run anything against a shared database or Integration Server; agents use throwaway local or CI instances only. Deploys happen only in pipelines, started by a human's merge or a human-run manual job.
